1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,178
Day 17 of the war.

2
00:00:02,178 --> 00:00:09,299
The United States formally asked its allies for military help to reopen the Strait of Hormuz.

3
00:00:09,299 --> 00:00:10,907
France refused first.

4
00:00:10,907 --> 00:00:14,200
Germany, Italy, Spain and Belgium followed.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:15,578
China refused too.

6
00:00:15,578 --> 00:00:24,153
Within two hours the call was over, and President Trump issued a sharply worded statement condemning the allies.

7
00:00:26,553 --> 00:00:32,737
South Korea, Japan and Australia also declined to join the Hormuz coalition.

8
00:00:32,737 --> 00:00:40,386
All three cited constitutional limits on sending forces abroad without parliamentary approval.

9
00:00:40,386 --> 00:00:47,790
Seoul pledged only $500,000 in humanitarian aid and position data for Korean-flagged ships.

10
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:53,486
Trump answered the allies with a declaration: America would win alone.

11
00:00:55,886 --> 00:00:57,507
Day 18, before dawn.

12
00:00:57,507 --> 00:01:03,831
With U.S. cooperation, Israel struck several senior Iranian officials at once.

13
00:01:03,831 --> 00:01:14,615
At 5:12 AM, Iranian state TV confirmed the deaths of former parliament speaker Ali Larijani and Basij commander Gholamreza Soleimani.

14
00:01:14,615 --> 00:01:19,318
The oldest channel in Iran's nuclear diplomacy had closed.

15
00:01:21,718 --> 00:01:26,392
Iran's retaliation began one hour and forty-two minutes later.

16
00:01:26,392 --> 00:01:31,819
Ballistic missiles hit central Ramat Gan, killing two Israeli civilians.

17
00:01:31,819 --> 00:01:37,096
That afternoon, Israel launched a ground invasion of southern Lebanon.

18
00:01:37,096 --> 00:01:44,183
UNICEF announced that more than 1,100 children had been killed or wounded since the war began.

19
00:01:46,583 --> 00:01:51,995
There were fourteen monitors, and every one of them turned red at once.

20
00:01:51,995 --> 00:01:57,331
National Security Adviser Brian Collins did not get up from his chair.

21
00:01:57,331 --> 00:02:03,047
The most dangerous moment in a war isn't when everyone jumps to their feet.

22
00:02:03,047 --> 00:02:04,724
It's when no one does.

23
00:02:04,724 --> 00:02:06,859
Confusion makes people move.

24
00:02:06,859 --> 00:02:08,840
Panic makes them go still.

25
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:12,434
“NATO's out, sir. Japan too. Korea. Australia.”

26
00:02:12,434 --> 00:02:14,575
His aide kept his voice low.

27
00:02:14,575 --> 00:02:16,410
Collins didn't even nod.

28
00:02:16,410 --> 00:02:18,780
An alliance was never a person.

29
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:24,667
An alliance was a system, and systems always flow toward their own interests.

30
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:27,726
A war you start alone, you finish alone.

31
00:02:30,126 --> 00:02:35,625
Foreign Minister Lee Junhyeok stood at the window, looking down at Seoul.

32
00:02:35,625 --> 00:02:40,221
People were walking to work, knowing nothing. He envied them.

33
00:02:40,221 --> 00:02:44,063
The cable from the U.S. Embassy was two lines long.

34
00:02:44,063 --> 00:02:47,001
A request to join the Hormuz operation.

35
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:50,315
An answer required within twenty-four hours.

36
00:02:50,315 --> 00:02:53,773
The reply had already gone out under his name.

37
00:02:53,773 --> 00:02:59,185
Formal regrets, constitutional constraints, a strong call for diplomacy.

38
00:02:59,185 --> 00:03:02,191
“Tell them there's no official reaction.

39
00:03:02,191 --> 00:03:06,024
We support it, but we won't take part. That's all.”

40
00:03:06,024 --> 00:03:14,142
When one nation leaves another alone on the battlefield, it is always done in a properly formatted document.

41
00:03:16,542 --> 00:03:18,619
The children were asleep.

42
00:03:18,619 --> 00:03:24,769
Fatima al-Rashid had put all three to bed and sat down in the living room.

43
00:03:24,769 --> 00:03:28,508
Since the war began, every sound had changed.

44
00:03:28,508 --> 00:03:31,250
An engine sounded like a missile.

45
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:33,992
Lightning felt like an explosion.

46
00:03:33,992 --> 00:03:40,723
That night, an Iranian drone struck an eight-story apartment building in Salmiya.

47
00:03:40,723 --> 00:03:42,223
The ceiling shook.

48
00:03:42,223 --> 00:03:47,223
Her youngest was crying — five years old, not even sure why.

49
00:03:47,223 --> 00:03:51,723
Something was frightening, and that was reason enough.

50
00:03:51,723 --> 00:03:55,223
Fatima held her and looked out the window.

51
00:03:55,223 --> 00:03:58,473
Smoke rose from the building next door.

52
00:03:58,473 --> 00:04:00,223
There were no lights.

53
00:04:00,223 --> 00:04:02,973
Wars are fought between soldiers.

54
00:04:02,973 --> 00:04:09,806
But in the darkness a war leaves behind, the ones who cry are always the children.

55
00:04:12,206 --> 00:04:14,907
Altitude: twelve thousand meters.

56
00:04:14,907 --> 00:04:18,346
The Israeli pilot checked the coordinates.

57
00:04:18,346 --> 00:04:20,147
Then he checked again.

58
00:04:20,147 --> 00:04:22,521
The target was not a soldier.

59
00:04:22,521 --> 00:04:28,825
But in war, the line between civilians and the powerful exists only on paper.

60
00:04:28,825 --> 00:04:35,703
He thought of his mother in Haifa — the woman who never wanted her son in this seat.

61
00:04:35,703 --> 00:04:40,977
The release button is pressed with two fingers. That's the rule.

62
00:04:40,977 --> 00:04:42,954
One finger is a mistake.

63
00:04:42,954 --> 00:04:45,509
Two fingers make it a decision.

64
00:04:45,509 --> 00:04:47,652
There were two explosions.

65
00:04:47,652 --> 00:04:49,959
The first took off the roof.

66
00:04:49,959 --> 00:04:52,266
No one remembers the second.

67
00:04:52,266 --> 00:04:56,222
Memory only reaches as far as the moment before.

68
00:04:58,622 --> 00:05:02,153
The siren had sounded six minutes earlier.

69
00:05:02,153 --> 00:05:06,861
David Levy, seventy-two, did not go down to the shelter.

70
00:05:06,861 --> 00:05:10,056
He had lived through 1967, 1973, 2006.

71
00:05:10,056 --> 00:05:12,494
He had heard too many sirens.

72
00:05:12,494 --> 00:05:14,428
He stood at his window.

73
00:05:14,428 --> 00:05:19,557
In the early morning, Ramat Gan glowed the color of a sunset.

74
00:05:19,557 --> 00:05:21,161
The glass trembled.

75
00:05:21,161 --> 00:05:24,791
The next moment, he was lying on the floor.

76
00:05:24,791 --> 00:05:27,746
The sound had gone out of his ears.

77
00:05:27,746 --> 00:05:32,812
The ceiling was cracked, and dust drifted down. This is war.

78
00:05:32,812 --> 00:05:35,683
Not because I didn't take shelter.

79
00:05:35,683 --> 00:05:38,722
Because the war came looking for me.

80
00:05:38,722 --> 00:05:41,002
Civilians never choose war.

81
00:05:41,002 --> 00:05:43,788
But war always chooses civilians.

82
00:05:46,188 --> 00:05:53,559
The treads of a Merkava tank were far quieter than people imagine. Silent. Ominously silent.

83
00:05:53,559 --> 00:06:01,572
For Mohammad Kasser at a Hezbollah lookout post, the tanks he couldn't see were the most terrifying.

84
00:06:01,572 --> 00:06:06,219
If you could see them, at least you knew which way to run.

85
00:06:06,219 --> 00:06:08,423
This was not an air strike.

86
00:06:08,423 --> 00:06:11,279
Air strikes came down from the sky.

87
00:06:11,279 --> 00:06:15,850
This came across the ground. Armor. Infantry. Artillery.

88
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:17,727
The oldest form of war.

89
00:06:17,727 --> 00:06:19,604
He picked up his AK-47.

90
00:06:19,604 --> 00:06:21,726
He couldn't shoot the sky.

91
00:06:21,726 --> 00:06:23,766
He couldn't stop a drone.

92
00:06:23,766 --> 00:06:26,052
A border is a line on a map.

93
00:06:26,052 --> 00:06:30,622
Tank treads roll right over it — and the line is erased.

94
00:06:33,022 --> 00:06:34,591
There was a number.

95
00:06:34,591 --> 00:06:36,655
More than eleven hundred.

96
00:06:36,655 --> 00:06:38,307
Two hundred in Iran.

97
00:06:38,307 --> 00:06:42,683
Ninety-one in Lebanon. Four in Israel. One in Kuwait.

98
00:06:42,683 --> 00:06:47,803
The report had no names in it. Only numbers. Numbers are tidy.

99
00:06:47,803 --> 00:06:49,867
Children's names are not.

100
00:06:49,867 --> 00:06:52,257
In Isfahan, Zahra was twelve.

101
00:06:52,257 --> 00:06:57,861
She fell on a stairwell while running for shelter and hurt her head.

102
00:06:57,861 --> 00:07:00,581
It wasn't a missile. It was fear.

103
00:07:00,581 --> 00:07:02,888
In Nabatieh, Youssef is six.

104
00:07:02,888 --> 00:07:06,761
He is alive, but his right ear no longer hears.

105
00:07:06,761 --> 00:07:10,305
Children don't know why anyone is fighting.

106
00:07:10,305 --> 00:07:12,036
Adults think they do.

107
00:07:12,036 --> 00:07:15,497
That difference is what makes all of this.

108
00:07:17,897 --> 00:07:23,724
Lieutenant Kim Junho watched the screens in the combat information center.

109
00:07:23,724 --> 00:07:29,944
A Korean Navy liaison officer, his job was to send Seoul what he saw and heard.

110
00:07:29,944 --> 00:07:34,275
Larijani's name appeared on the list of confirmed dead.

111
00:07:34,275 --> 00:07:40,260
“I hear Trump went after Korea too?” asked U.S. Navy officer Mark Henderson.

112
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:43,203
“No official reaction from our side.”

113
00:07:43,203 --> 00:07:45,589
Henderson gave a bitter laugh.

114
00:07:45,589 --> 00:07:47,021
“That's diplomacy.

115
00:07:47,021 --> 00:07:51,634
Saying nothing is still saying something.” Kim didn't nod.

116
00:07:51,634 --> 00:07:57,759
On a carrier before dawn, he wasn't sure saying nothing was really all right.

117
00:07:57,759 --> 00:07:59,827
Watching was a choice too.

118
00:07:59,827 --> 00:08:03,167
And every choice came with responsibility.

119
00:08:05,567 --> 00:08:08,052
In Tehran, Larijani was dead.

120
00:08:08,052 --> 00:08:11,480
In Ramat Gan, two civilians were killed.

121
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:15,421
In southern Lebanon, tanks crossed the border.

122
00:08:15,421 --> 00:08:17,563
In Kuwait, a child cried.

123
00:08:17,563 --> 00:08:21,076
In Seoul, a minister looked out a window.

124
00:08:21,076 --> 00:08:33,672
And millions of people, watching the news, turned off their screens — not because it was unbearable, but because they had seen too much to feel it.

125
00:08:33,672 --> 00:08:41,540
Rumi's Masnavi begins: “Listen to the reed, how it tells a tale, complaining of separation.”

126
00:08:41,540 --> 00:08:47,013
A reed only makes a sound once it's been torn from the reed bed.

127
00:08:47,013 --> 00:08:53,512
Whether that sound is a melody or a scream depends on the ear that hears it.

128
00:08:53,512 --> 00:08:57,104
The cruelest moment of war isn't the bomb.

129
00:08:57,104 --> 00:08:59,498
It's when we get used to it.

130
00:08:59,498 --> 00:09:01,126
— End of Episode 6.

131
00:09:01,126 --> 00:09:05,153
Continued in Episode 7, “Fire at South Pars.” —

